data: {"type":"result","result":{"summary":{"overview":"Apollo 11 in July 1969 landed the first humans on the Moon, the culmination of a Cold War Space Race that began with Sputnik.","sections":[{"heading":"Apollo 11 Mission","points":[{"text":"Commander Neil Armstrong, Michael Collins, and Buzz Aldrin flew Apollo 11, launched from Kennedy Space Center on July 16, 1969.","points":["1.2"],"chunks":[1]},{"text":"Armstrong and Aldrin landed the Eagle in the Sea of Tranquility on July 20 while Collins orbited in Columbia.","points":["1.5"],"chunks":[1]},{"text":"Armstrong became the first human to walk on the Moon, followed by Aldrin nineteen minutes later.","points":[],"chunks":[2]},{"text":"After more than 21 hours on the surface, the crew rejoined Collins in lunar orbit and splashed down July 24.","points":["2.3"],"chunks":[2]}]},{"heading":"Origins of the Space Race","points":[{"text":"The Soviet Union launched Sputnik 1 on October 4, 1957, the first artificial satellite, sparking the Space Race.","points":["4.1","4.3"],"chunks":[4]},{"text":"Yuri Gagarin became the first person in space on April 12, 1961, and Alan Shepard the first American on May 5.","points":["4.5","4.6"],"chunks":[4]},{"text":"Khrushchev declined Kennedy's June 1961 proposal for a joint Moon landing project with the Soviet Union.","points":["7.4"],"chunks":[7]}]},{"heading":"Kennedy's Moon Goal","points":[{"text":"On May 25, 1961, Kennedy told Congress the nation should land a man on the Moon and return him safely before the decade ended.","points":["5.3","2.5"],"chunks":[2,5]},{"text":"Kennedy said the Moon challenge would be more difficult and expensive but more important than any other long-range space project.","points":["5.4"],"chunks":[5]},{"text":"In his 1962 Rice University speech, Kennedy called space conquest worthy of mankind's best, repeating 'We choose to go to the Moon.'","points":["6.3","6.5"],"chunks":[6]},{"text":"Kennedy said the Moon challenge was chosen to organize and measure the best energies and skills of the nation.","points":[],"chunks":[7]}]},{"heading":"Spacecraft Design and Setbacks","points":[{"text":"NASA head James Webb announced in July 1962 that lunar orbit rendezvous would be used for Apollo.","points":[],"chunks":[8]},{"text":"The lunar module had a descent stage for landing on the Moon and an ascent stage to return astronauts to lunar orbit.","points":["8.4"],"chunks":[8]},{"text":"The January 1967 Apollo 1 fire killed three astronauts, and its investigation abruptly halted Project Apollo.","points":[],"chunks":[9]}]},{"heading":"Testing and Legacy","points":[{"text":"Apollo 7 through 10 tested Apollo hardware in Earth and lunar orbit before Apollo 11's flight.","points":[],"chunks":[9]},{"text":"Armstrong's moonwalk was broadcast live to an estimated 600 million viewers, about one-fifth of the world's population.","points":["3.2"],"chunks":[3]},{"text":"The Soviet Luna 15 crashed on the Moon on July 21 while Armstrong and Aldrin were on the surface.","points":[],"chunks":[3]},{"text":"The crew received ticker-tape parades, a world goodwill tour, and the Presidential Medal of Freedom.","points":["3.5"],"chunks":[3]}]},{"heading":"Also central in the text","points":[{"text":"Apollo 11 (July 16–24, 1969) was the American spaceflight that first landed humans on the Moon and the fifth crewed mission of NASA's Apollo program.","points":["1.1"],"chunks":[1]},{"text":"Armstrong was the first human to walk on the Moon about six hours after landing, with Aldrin following nineteen minutes later.","points":["2.1"],"chunks":[2]},{"text":"Apollo 11 was the culmination of the Cold War Space Race between the United States and the Soviet Union.","points":["2.4"],"chunks":[2]}]}]},"coverage":{"covered":[1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9],"missing":[],"noPoints":[],"citedPoints":["1.1","1.2","1.5","2.1","2.3","2.4","2.5","3.2","3.5","4.1","4.3","4.5","4.6","5.3","5.4","6.3","6.5","7.4","8.4"],"uncitedPoints":["1.3","1.4","2.2","3.1","3.3","3.4","4.2","4.4","5.1","5.2","5.5","6.1","6.2","6.4","7.1","7.2","7.3","7.5","7.6","8.1","8.2","8.3","8.5","9.1","9.2","9.3","9.4","9.5"],"unchecked":[]},"entities":["Apollo 11","NASA","Apollo program","Neil Armstrong","Michael Collins","Edwin 'Buzz' Aldrin","Saturn V","Kennedy Space Center","Command Module (CM)","Service Module (SM)","Lunar Module (LM)","Eagle","Armstrong","Aldrin","Collins","Richard Nixon","Space Race","United States","Soviet Union","John F. Kennedy","Apollo 1","Mercury","Gemini","Luna 15","Buzz Aldrin","Columbia","National Air and Space Museum","Presidential Medal of Freedom","Sputnik 1","Sputnik crisis","Dwight D. Eisenhower","National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA)","Project Mercury","Yuri Gagarin","Alan Shepard","Kennedy","Eisenhower","United States Congress","Earth","Moon","Rice University","Houston, Texas","Atlantic","Texas","Norbert Wiener","Massachusetts Institute of Technology","Project Apollo","Nikita Khrushchev","United Nations General Assembly","lunar orbit rendezvous","direct ascent","Earth orbit rendezvous","Gus Grissom","Ed White","Roger B. Chaffee","Apollo 7","Apollo 8","Apollo 9","Apollo 10"],"retries":0,"chunkCount":9,"findingCount":9,"keyPoints":[{"chunk":1,"points":[{"id":"1.1","text":"Apollo 11 (July 16–24, 1969) was the American spaceflight that first landed humans on the Moon and the fifth crewed mission of NASA's Apollo program."},{"id":"1.2","text":"The crew was Commander Neil Armstrong, Command Module Pilot Michael Collins, and Lunar Module Pilot Edwin 'Buzz' Aldrin, each on their second and final spaceflight."},{"id":"1.3","text":"The Saturn V launched from Kennedy Space Center in Florida on July 16, 1969 at 13:32 UTC."},{"id":"1.4","text":"The command module housed the three astronauts and was the only part to return to Earth; the service module provided propulsion, electrical power, oxygen, and water."},{"id":"1.5","text":"Armstrong and Aldrin landed the LM Eagle in the Sea of Tranquility on July 20 at 20:17 UTC while Collins remained in lunar orbit aboard CM Columbia."}]},{"chunk":2,"points":[{"id":"2.1","text":"Armstrong was the first human to walk on the Moon about six hours after landing, with Aldrin following nineteen minutes later."},{"id":"2.2","text":"The crew spent about two and a half hours on the surface, planting a flag, speaking with President Nixon, deploying instruments, and collecting 21.5 kg of lunar material."},{"id":"2.3","text":"After more than 21 hours on the surface, Armstrong and Aldrin rejoined Collins in lunar orbit; the crew splashed down in the Pacific on July 24."},{"id":"2.4","text":"Apollo 11 was the culmination of the Cold War Space Race between the United States and the Soviet Union."},{"id":"2.5","text":"President John F. Kennedy set the goal in May 1961 of landing a man on the Moon and returning him safely before the decade ended; the program recovered from the fatal Apollo 1 fire in January 1967."}]},{"chunk":3,"points":[{"id":"3.1","text":"The Soviet Union could not match the Saturn V rocket, and its uncrewed Luna 15 crashed on the Moon on July 21 while Armstrong and Aldrin were on the surface."},{"id":"3.2","text":"Armstrong's moonwalk was broadcast live to an estimated 600 million viewers, roughly one-fifth of the world's population."},{"id":"3.3","text":"Stepping onto the lunar surface, Armstrong declared: \"That's one small step for [a] man, one giant leap for mankind.\""},{"id":"3.4","text":"Lunar samples led to the identification of three previously unrecognized minerals, and surface instruments kept returning data for years."},{"id":"3.5","text":"The crew received ticker-tape parades, a world goodwill tour, and the Presidential Medal of Freedom; Columbia is preserved at the National Air and Space Museum, while Eagle's descent stage remains on the Moon."}]},{"chunk":4,"points":[{"id":"4.1","text":"On October 4, 1957, the Soviet Union launched Sputnik 1, the first artificial satellite, which surprised the world and fueled fears about Soviet capabilities."},{"id":"4.2","text":"Sputnik's success suggested the USSR could potentially deliver nuclear weapons over intercontinental distances, challenging American claims of superiority."},{"id":"4.3","text":"Sputnik sparked the Sputnik crisis and ignited the Space Race between the two superpowers."},{"id":"4.4","text":"President Eisenhower responded by creating NASA and initiating Project Mercury, which aimed to place a man into Earth orbit."},{"id":"4.5","text":"On April 12, 1961, cosmonaut Yuri Gagarin became the first person in space and the first to orbit the Earth."},{"id":"4.6","text":"On May 5, 1961, Alan Shepard became the first American in space, on a 15-minute suborbital flight."}]},{"chunk":5,"points":[{"id":"5.1","text":"Kennedy, Eisenhower's successor, chose a challenge beyond existing rocket capabilities so both countries would start from an equal position."},{"id":"5.2","text":"A crewed Moon mission was presented as the challenge that would serve this purpose."},{"id":"5.3","text":"On May 25, 1961, Kennedy told Congress the nation should commit to landing a man on the Moon and returning him safely before the 1960s end."},{"id":"5.4","text":"Kennedy said this project would be more impressive and important for long-range space exploration than any other, and also more difficult and expensive."},{"id":"5.5","text":"Kennedy proposed accelerating lunar spacecraft development, developing larger liquid and solid fuel boosters, and funding engines and unmanned exploration."}]},{"chunk":6,"points":[{"id":"6.1","text":"Kennedy says reaching the Moon will be a national effort, since all Americans must work to put one man there."},{"id":"6.2","text":"On September 12, 1962, Kennedy gave a speech to about 40,000 people at Rice University football stadium in Houston, Texas."},{"id":"6.3","text":"Kennedy argues space has no strife, prejudice, or national conflict yet, and its conquest deserves the best of all mankind."},{"id":"6.4","text":"Kennedy answers the question of why the Moon by comparing it to climbing mountains, flying the Atlantic, and Rice playing Texas."},{"id":"6.5","text":"The speech's refrain is 'We choose to go to the Moon,' widely quoted from its middle portion."}]},{"chunk":7,"points":[{"id":"7.1","text":"Kennedy said the US chooses to go to the Moon this decade because the challenge is hard, to organize and measure the best energies and skills."},{"id":"7.2","text":"The proposed Moon program faced widespread opposition; mathematician Norbert Wiener at MIT dubbed it a \"moondoggle.\""},{"id":"7.3","text":"The Moon landing effort was already named Project Apollo."},{"id":"7.4","text":"Kennedy proposed a joint Moon landing project to Soviet Premier Nikita Khrushchev in June 1961, but Khrushchev declined."},{"id":"7.5","text":"Kennedy proposed a joint Moon expedition again at a UN General Assembly speech on September 20, 1963; the joint idea was abandoned after his death."},{"id":"7.6","text":"An early crucial decision was choosing lunar orbit rendezvous over direct ascent and Earth orbit rendezvous."}]},{"chunk":8,"points":[{"id":"8.1","text":"In July 1962, NASA head James Webb announced lunar orbit rendezvous would be used for Apollo, with three major spacecraft parts."},{"id":"8.2","text":"The command module held a cabin for three astronauts and was the only part that returned to Earth."},{"id":"8.3","text":"The service module supplied the command module with propulsion, electrical power, oxygen, and water."},{"id":"8.4","text":"The lunar module had two stages: a descent stage for landing on the Moon and an ascent stage to return astronauts to lunar orbit."},{"id":"8.5","text":"The design allowed launch by a single Saturn V rocket, and Project Gemini developed technologies and techniques Apollo required."}]},{"chunk":9,"points":[{"id":"9.1","text":"The Apollo 1 fire on January 27, 1967 killed astronauts Gus Grissom, Ed White, and Roger B. Chaffee, and the investigation that followed abruptly halted Project Apollo."},{"id":"9.2","text":"In October 1968, Apollo 7 evaluated the command module in Earth orbit."},{"id":"9.3","text":"In December 1968, Apollo 8 tested the command module in lunar orbit."},{"id":"9.4","text":"In March 1969, Apollo 9 tested the lunar module in Earth orbit; in May, Apollo 10 conducted a 'dress rehearsal' in lunar orbit."},{"id":"9.5","text":"By July 1969, everything was ready for Apollo 11 to make the final step onto the Moon."}]}],"reviewFlags":["1.1","2.1","2.4"],"review":"done","notice":null,"retryOutcome":"none","metrics":{"totalMs":17250,"totalTokens":27262,"totalCost":0.0063218,"priceNote":null,"cheapCost":0.004357,"cheapCalls":10,"synthesisCost":0.0019648,"unreportedCalls":0}}}
